TI  - Dosimetry and chemical yield analysis of metal nanoparticles in FLASH radiotherapy: a Monte Carlo study.
AU  - Fardirad S
AU  - Ghasemizad A
AU  - Abolfath R
PY  - 2025
JO  - Physics in medicine and biology
AB  - Objective.To investigate the influence of high-Zmetallic nanoparticles (NPs) (Au, Ag, Pt) influence both physical dose deposition and radiochemical yields (⋅OHand H2O2) under conventional dose rate (CDR) and ultra-high dose rate (FLASH) irradiation, and to explore whether their presence modulates the FLASH effect, either enhancing or suppressing it depending on context.Approach.Monte Carlo simulations were performed using the TOol for PArticle Simulation-nBio/Geant4-DNA toolkit to model water radiolysis in the presence of a 50 nm metallic NP. Electron and photon beams of 1 MeV were delivered under multi-source configurations with varying inter-source distances, representing both CDR and FLASH-like conditions. The evolution of chemical species was tracked from 1 ps to 1 ms, andG-values for⋅OHand H2O2were extracted.Main results.NPs increased local energy deposition through the emission of low-energy secondary electrons; however, chemical yields exhibited nonlinear and configuration-dependent behavior. Under FLASH-like conditions, H2O2yields were consistently reduced, and this suppression was further modulated by NP type and source geometry. In some cases, NPs acted as radiosensitizers (increased⋅OHyields), while in others they showed radioprotective trends (reduced⋅OHand H2O2). These effects stem from competing mechanisms including radical-radical recombination, scavenging at NP surfaces, and spatial heterogeneity of secondary electron distributions (based on the inter-track and intra-track analysis).Significance.This work demonstrates that NP-assisted FLASH radiotherapy involves a complex interplay between physical and chemical processes, where increased dose deposition does not always translate into higher radiochemical yields. The dual role of NPs-as both potential radiosensitizers and radioprotectors-underscores the need for careful parameter optimization and experimental validation before clinical translation of NP-enhanced FLASH protocols.
