AB  - Objective. FLASH radiotherapy (FLASH-RT) with ultra-high dose rate (UHDR) irradiation (i.e. > 40 Gy s-1) spares the function of normal tissues while preserving antitumor efficacy, known as the FLASH effect. The biological effects after conventional dose rate-radiotherapy (CONV-RT) with ≤0.1 Gy s-1have been well modeled by considering microdosimetry and DNA repair processes, meanwhile modeling of radiosensitivities under UHDR irradiation is insufficient. Here, we developed anintegrated microdosimetric-kinetic(IMK)model for UHDR-irradiationenabling the prediction of surviving fraction after UHDR irradiation.Approach.TheIMK model for UHDR-irradiationconsiders the initial DNA damage yields by the modification of indirect effects under UHDR compared to CONV dose rate. The developed model is based on the linear-quadratic (LQ) nature with the dose and dose square coefficients, considering the reduction of DNA damage yields as a function of dose rate.Main results.The estimate by the developed model could successfully reproduce thein vitroexperimental dose-response curve for various cell line types and dose rates.Significance.The developed model would be useful for predicting the biological effects under the UHDR irradiation.
